  "//large_theta_rungs": "Rungs 44-46 + the two sim_binomial_bigsd_agq_k* goldens: the large-theta-hat regime. Rung 44 sim_binomial_bigsd is Bernoulli, scalar (1|g), 300 groups x 6 obs, fitted theta-hat 4.5106 -- 3.4x the corpus's previous GLMM maximum (1.339, VerbAgg); rung 45 sim_poisson_bigsd is the count-family counterpart at 2.9716. Both are Laplace (nAGQ=1) curated rungs on the EXISTING tiers, not a new tier and not an expansion of the curated 6-rung cross-engine oracle (design 6). Rung 46 sim_sparse_binomial_bigsd is the SPARSE arm of the same regime -- one primary grouping g1 (300 groups x 12) plus seven crossed intercept-only extras c1-c7, fitted theta-hat(g1) 3.9076 -- added because rungs 44-45 are both dense-routed scalar (1|g), so the sparse solver's large-theta-hat behavior was untested rather than merely uncalibrated (A4 A2.0a batch); it is a 2-way gate (glmm vs lme4 only, see its own '//' comment above for why). Their data comes from prep/gen_large_theta_data.R, the third of the five prep scripts (export_data.R rungs 1-28, gen_weights_data.R 29-43, gen_illcond_data.R, gen_scale_data.R). THE THIRD DATASET OF THAT SPEC, sim_binomial_zerosd (theta = 0 exactly), IS DELIBERATELY NOT A RUNG: lme4's vcov(m, use.hessian = TRUE) HARD-ERRORS on a boundary fit ('Hessian is unavailable' -- m@optinfo$derivs is NULL), which is exactly what engines/lme4.R:269 and :146 call, so adding it as a curated rung would abort the whole oracle run. What it exists to gate is a FLAG divergence, not a number -- lme4 warns 'boundary (singular) fit' (so engines/lme4.R records converged = FALSE) while glmm pins the component and reports converged = true with singular = true, and BOTH land on a bit-exact 0.0 stddev, so compare.R's numeric gates would see nothing. It is gated in-crate instead, by glmm_zerosd_boundary_reports_converged_and_singular in src/fit/glmm_tests.rs, which needs no oracle JSON. Verified 2026-07-30 that rel_max(0.0, 0.0) = 0 on this dataset, i.e. the two engines do not straddle rel_max's 1e-12 denominator floor.",
  "//gamma_rungs": "Rung 23 (sim_gamma, dense) RE-LANDED after the B1 varcorr sigma-hat^2 fix. Rung 24 (sim_sparse_gamma) RE-LANDED 2026-07-21: the backed-out failure was DIAGNOSED AND FIXED by the frontend RE-ordering change -- the parser used to extract slope REs before intercept REs, so the harness lowered (1|gp)+(1+x1..x4|ge) with ge as PRIMARY (q_p=5) and routed the DENSE kernel, whose joint optimizer lands ~2e-3 deviance above the tolPwrss=1e-13 lme4 optimum (beta[0] 1.9e-2 off). The frontend now lowers random effects in FORMULA ORDER (nested terms still sort first -- NestedWithin is relative to the primary), so gp is primary, the model routes SPARSE, and it lands 2e-4 above lme4 -- matching the in-crate golden that hand-builds the same orientation. Tolerances were NOT relaxed. STILL OPEN (recorded, not a rung gap): the dense joint optimizer's ~2e-3 shortfall on a q_p=5 primary with an extra grouping is real and reachable by writing the slope term first; the ordering fix stops ROUTING rung 24 there, it does not improve that path (dense stage-1/2 schedule work is a separate item). These are rung-18-style 2-WAY gates (no jl_formula): MixedModels' Gamma GLMM is a documented different estimator, its JSONs stay intentionally absent. See validation/README.md. GAMMA-INVERSE WITH RANDOM EFFECTS, SPARSE: attempted 2026-07-21 as spec sim_sparse_gamma_inv over the sim_sparse_gamma data and ABANDONED -- no oracle is obtainable there. That data is simulated under a LOG link and spans y 0.0045..265, so an inverse-link model is misspecified for it: eta goes negative, mu = 1/eta with it, and the Gamma likelihood is undefined. lme4 dies with 'PIRLS loop resulted in NaN value', and so does a plain fixed-only stats::glm on the same formula -- the failure is the data, not the mixed solver. Oracling this cell needs a dataset simulated under the inverse link, which is a new simulated fixture and a separate decision. The DENSE Gamma-inverse mixed cell is covered: sim_gamma_inv_glmm fits cleanly on sim_gamma.",
  "//agq_field": "`agq: <k>` marks the datasets AGQ can be run on, for the OPT-IN timing pass only (VALIDATION_AGQ, read by engines/lme4.R and engines/glmm.rs). It is inert on a normal run: the curated cross-engine sweep stays pinned at nAGQ=1 (engines/lme4.R's fit_call) and the AGQ pass writes to results/{lme4,glmm}_agq_*, which compare.R's results/lme4_{empirical,simulated}/*.json glob never sees -- design 6, 'the curated 6-rung oracle is NOT expanded'. Eligibility is glmm's AGQ gate (src/glmm/deviance.rs): binomial/Poisson, ONE grouping factor, q <= 3. Arabidopsis and sim_poisson_nested are excluded for having two groupings. The three vector-RE entries (sim_binomial_slope1/sim_poisson_slope1 q=2, sim_binomial_slope2 q=3) are glmm-only -- glmer refuses nAGQ>1 for vector REs, so lme4.R skips them and their rows carry no lme4 time.",
  "//m3_goldens": "M3 family/link/AGQ reference fits for the IN-CRATE frozen goldens (NOT curated rungs). Generated by engines/goldens_agq.R into validation/goldens/<name>.json -- a SEPARATE dir so compare.R's results/<engine>/*.json glob (the curated cross-engine sweep) is untouched, per design 6 'the curated 6-rung oracle is NOT expanded'. lme4/MASS only; no Julia/Rust cross-check. Every golden in goldens/ must have an entry here and therefore a generator -- sleepstudy_lmm, penicillin_lmm and pastes_lmm sat in goldens/ unregistered and unregenerable until 2026-07-21; they are the SAME datasets as rungs 2/3/4 but distinct model specs (pastes_lmm is the nested (1|batch/cask) form, rung 4 is the flat idiom), which is why they are separate goldens rather than rung results. Their files previously predated this script and carried its predecessor's schema (reml instead of nagq, 12-sig-fig rounding, and pastes_lmm's varcomp blocks in the opposite order to the cask:batch-first order lme4 emits today). Regenerating them reproduced every estimate (penicillin stddev to 6e-7, the rounding; pastes to 1e-7 once the two blocks are matched by name), so the regenerated form was ADOPTED 2026-07-21 and all three are now byte-reproducible from this script. Adopting it required first converting every multi-block varcomp read in the crate to key by group NAME rather than index: glmm emits declaration order, lme4's VarCorr emits descending level count, and pastes_lmm is the fixture where the two disagree.",
